WEBVTT

00:00:00.010 --> 00:00:01.550
What's happening right now is a big deal.

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:03.520
This is the biggest launch since ChatGPT.

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:07.770
It's called Muse. "Muse." "Muse."
"Has the tech world buzzing." "5

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:13.070
million downloads in 22 days."
"Surpassing ChatGPT" And he built it.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.849
Most people are using AI to
answer questions, basically

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:17.130
glorified Google search.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.810
This is something totally different.

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:23.380
"It's an AI agent" "Built for everyday
life." "We believe that Muse will

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:26.499
make you money." "One guy had Muse
help plan a weekend away with his

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.389
wife." It can make phone calls.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:30.009
It can do tasks for you.

00:00:30.009 --> 00:00:34.249
But for it to work, you have to trust
it, first with your personal information,

00:00:34.549 --> 00:00:40.249
then to do what you actually want it to
do, and then that it's not gonna, well,

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:44.549
get out of control, all at a time when
the most important AI leaders are warning

00:00:44.549 --> 00:00:47.639
us that AI is getting out of control.

00:00:47.719 --> 00:00:49.889
And I urge you to urge me to stop.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.799
If you understand what's happening
right now with Muse, you'll understand

00:00:53.849 --> 00:00:55.809
the biggest things happening in AI.

00:00:56.219 --> 00:00:57.709
So what can it actually do?

00:00:57.959 --> 00:00:59.129
When should we trust it?

00:00:59.379 --> 00:01:03.479
And what do we need to do to
make sure this goes right?

00:01:03.859 --> 00:01:06.619
And we're at the moment
of, like, peak insanity.

00:01:06.639 --> 00:01:09.369
Why are all these billionaires
obsessed with restaurant reservations?

00:01:09.479 --> 00:01:12.439
We have a shared interest in
making sure this technology

00:01:12.479 --> 00:01:13.589
does not go horribly wrong.

00:01:13.729 --> 00:01:16.559
Is that something that's happening
in some private room somewhere?

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:21.839
The degree to which certain personal
animosities are actually defining

00:01:21.869 --> 00:01:25.049
how they're developing this really
important technology is insane to me.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.528
Amazing Let's do this.

00:01:33.668 --> 00:01:34.348
Okay.
Welcome.

00:01:34.618 --> 00:01:35.268
How have you been?

00:01:35.368 --> 00:01:35.848
I'm good.

00:01:35.848 --> 00:01:36.458
I'm really good.

00:01:36.468 --> 00:01:37.228
How have you been?

00:01:37.738 --> 00:01:40.018
This is- It's been kind of insane, yeah.

00:01:40.078 --> 00:01:40.718
Yeah.

00:01:41.288 --> 00:01:43.377
What does it feel like?

00:01:43.758 --> 00:01:48.668
We didn't expect that it would be such
a hit, so I feel amazing for, like, the

00:01:48.668 --> 00:01:51.538
whole team and everyone who, like, worked
so hard on it 'cause it's, like, it's

00:01:51.538 --> 00:01:52.828
really come together and become a thing.

00:01:53.428 --> 00:01:55.088
So… That's what I'm
excited to talk about.

00:01:55.198 --> 00:01:56.647
I know you know all
about the show already.

00:01:57.138 --> 00:02:00.328
But this isn't, like, a
traditional interview.

00:02:00.628 --> 00:02:02.207
Like, I'm not gonna ask about financials.

00:02:02.208 --> 00:02:03.488
I'm not gonna ask about management style.

00:02:03.718 --> 00:02:09.668
You just launched one of the most popular
consumer tools of all time, and so the

00:02:09.758 --> 00:02:13.957
only thing that I wanna know is what's
the future that you're working toward

00:02:13.957 --> 00:02:18.867
with that tool, and how can this audience
that's really optimistic and really wants

00:02:18.867 --> 00:02:23.348
to be part of making sure that that future
go right, how can they feel like they're

00:02:23.468 --> 00:02:25.597
part of the decisions that are being made?

00:02:26.238 --> 00:02:26.718
Yep.

00:02:26.718 --> 00:02:27.267
All of that.

00:02:27.267 --> 00:02:27.891
Yeah.
That's what we do.

00:02:28.087 --> 00:02:31.977
One of the reasons I wanted
to talk to you was I've always

00:02:31.977 --> 00:02:34.057
appreciated that you're really blunt.

00:02:36.827 --> 00:02:42.368
Like, I, I honestly think that
people need straight talk about this.

00:02:42.388 --> 00:02:46.088
I think that they feel confused,
and I wanna get at what's really

00:02:46.088 --> 00:02:51.128
happening, what have you built, how
are people using it, how are they

00:02:51.238 --> 00:02:53.467
not, what are the real challenges.

00:02:54.017 --> 00:02:57.217
I think one other interesting
thing is given how popular Muse

00:02:57.217 --> 00:03:03.757
has become, I want to use it as a way
to talk about other things in AI.

00:03:04.348 --> 00:03:04.707
Awesome.

00:03:04.707 --> 00:03:05.037
Okay.

00:03:05.037 --> 00:03:05.617
Let's get into it.

00:03:06.057 --> 00:03:09.207
Muse is the fastest-growing
consumer tool since ChatGPT.

00:03:09.327 --> 00:03:13.658
Faster than ChatGPT, I think, and it
feels like this is the moment when

00:03:13.687 --> 00:03:18.927
we're moving maybe from chatbots to
agents, but there have been a lot of

00:03:18.937 --> 00:03:23.427
other popular agents, mostly in code,
and I'm curious, why do you think

00:03:23.427 --> 00:03:24.857
this is happening for Muse right now?

00:03:24.907 --> 00:03:26.407
Why is Muse exploding like this?

00:03:26.937 --> 00:03:31.197
I think one of the mysteries of AI so far
this year, for most of the year, has been

00:03:31.197 --> 00:03:36.817
that agents were happening for engineers
and software engineers and coders, and

00:03:36.817 --> 00:03:41.128
then nobody else in the world, like, was
experiencing it, knew what it was, like,

00:03:41.178 --> 00:03:42.708
understood exactly what was happening.

00:03:42.988 --> 00:03:47.347
And so for most of this year, you've
kind of had this, like, two dialogues

00:03:47.348 --> 00:03:52.087
almost where people who do code and
could see how powerful AI was becoming

00:03:52.087 --> 00:03:56.558
and, and how useful it was becoming were
like, "Oh my gosh, this is incredible."

00:03:56.717 --> 00:03:59.218
And then everyone else was kind of
like, "What are you talking about?

00:03:59.247 --> 00:04:00.278
I just still use a chatbot.

00:04:00.288 --> 00:04:03.147
Like, it's, like, a little bit better
than Google, but what are you guys all

00:04:03.147 --> 00:04:07.987
freaked out about?" And, um, and one of
the things that we were really excited

00:04:07.988 --> 00:04:12.468
to do is how do we take the magic that
engineers and coders and developers

00:04:12.468 --> 00:04:15.497
are seeing and bring that to everyone?

00:04:15.538 --> 00:04:18.187
One of the things that, that has
happened over the course of the past

00:04:18.187 --> 00:04:22.497
year is, like, the AI models started
out as pretty janky agents, and you

00:04:22.497 --> 00:04:25.788
kind of had to struggle through them
to get them to work, and then they

00:04:25.788 --> 00:04:27.677
slowly got better and better and better.

00:04:27.937 --> 00:04:31.580
And most of the industry's been really
focused on making them just, like- Better

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:33.100
and better and better at coding.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:36.320
But we really felt this was like a s-
a big gap because most people in the

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:38.000
world, they don't know how to code.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.769
They d- probably never even want to code.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:45.420
Um, but they still wanna feel the
experience of agents and, um, having

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:50.020
AI that is able to do more for you and
actually start taking on more end-to-end

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:52.370
workflows and take on more tasks for you.

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:56.400
So that was really the goal behind
Muse, and I think we spent a lot of time

00:04:56.900 --> 00:05:02.100
polishing all the little details to make
sure that it would just work for people

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.939
because we knew that, like, developers
can kind of withstand a lot of jank

00:05:07.209 --> 00:05:10.430
and a lot of stuff breaking and, like,
they can kind of deal with all of that

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:12.110
because, you know, they're, they're…

00:05:12.139 --> 00:05:12.760
It's technology.

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:15.500
They're used to dealing with, you
know- Yeah … unripe technology.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:18.579
But for everyone, you have to make
something that's really smooth

00:05:18.799 --> 00:05:20.239
and, and really works for them.

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:26.729
That also helps explain the big gap, I
think, between what it feels like for most

00:05:26.729 --> 00:05:31.239
people to actually use AI, when they were
using a chatbot in particular, and the

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:35.659
conversation around AI that's it's gonna
take everyone's jobs, it's really scary.

00:05:35.659 --> 00:05:38.740
It's, you know, there's a, there's a macro
conversation happening, and then there's

00:05:38.749 --> 00:05:40.370
an experience that people are having.

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:43.009
And it just seems like those things
are really, really far apart.

00:05:43.289 --> 00:05:47.549
And to your point, Muse is giving
more people the opportunity to have

00:05:47.549 --> 00:05:51.768
the insight that computer engineers
had, you know, call it a year ago.

00:05:52.238 --> 00:05:52.938
Yeah.
Is that fair?

00:05:53.078 --> 00:05:54.097
I, I think that's true, yeah.

00:05:54.128 --> 00:05:54.618
Yeah.

00:05:54.618 --> 00:05:57.688
There are also moments in history
that I think feel similar.

00:05:57.777 --> 00:06:01.517
Would you say it's like the
mobile phone moment for agents?

00:06:01.568 --> 00:06:05.568
There are, you know, billions of people
brought online by mobile phones that

00:06:05.657 --> 00:06:09.358
never had PCs because it was easy and
simple to use, and they didn't, you

00:06:09.358 --> 00:06:13.077
know. I actually hadn't thought about
that until you just mentioned it, but I

00:06:13.087 --> 00:06:14.637
think it's a great analogy because it's

00:06:15.147 --> 00:06:17.147
These models, they're improving
really fast, and they're

00:06:17.157 --> 00:06:18.268
improving really, really quickly.

00:06:18.507 --> 00:06:22.618
And it's really important that we,
as those models get better, that

00:06:22.817 --> 00:06:27.927
the, the superpowers that they, they
lend can be given to, like, literally

00:06:27.957 --> 00:06:29.327
everyone all around the world.

00:06:29.378 --> 00:06:33.647
The part that I like about the analogy
is, like, um, so much of it is just

00:06:33.648 --> 00:06:39.697
about figuring out how to package it all
together into a form factor that, um,

00:06:39.727 --> 00:06:42.897
will resonate with people, and that's
what we spent a lot of time trying to

00:06:42.897 --> 00:06:45.388
craft and get right and, and make perfect.

00:06:45.507 --> 00:06:49.768
Which also gets to one of the interesting
initial reactions that I've heard

00:06:49.827 --> 00:06:53.107
of Muse, which is, is everybody's
life more complicated than mine?

00:06:53.518 --> 00:06:57.407
Like, I wake up, I go to work,
I play with my kids, I have one

00:06:57.407 --> 00:07:01.418
restaurant reservation a month, I
plan one vacation with my spouse.

00:07:01.427 --> 00:07:04.608
Like, why are all these billionaires
obsessed with restaurant reservations?

00:07:04.997 --> 00:07:06.427
Why do I need an assistant?

00:07:07.047 --> 00:07:08.578
How would you answer that?

00:07:08.657 --> 00:07:12.127
Whenever I explain Muse to someone
just who knows nothing about it,

00:07:12.427 --> 00:07:15.698
um, I just say, like, Muse can help
save you money and save you time,

00:07:16.117 --> 00:07:20.207
and everybody in the world wants to
save money and wants to save time.

00:07:20.427 --> 00:07:24.547
Depending on what your lifestyle is,
that can mean a different thing to you.

00:07:24.808 --> 00:07:28.328
I think almost everyone has, like,
subscriptions that they shouldn't be

00:07:28.328 --> 00:07:31.907
paying for or subscriptions that are
really expensive, like cable bills or

00:07:31.907 --> 00:07:36.587
insurance, and for a lot of people, their
magic moment with Muse is just saving

00:07:36.597 --> 00:07:39.747
money on things that they shouldn't be
spending money on or they shouldn't be

00:07:39.747 --> 00:07:43.507
spending so much money on, or finding
gift cards that they hadn't spent or,

00:07:43.647 --> 00:07:45.268
you know, the list goes on and on and on.

00:07:45.337 --> 00:07:47.397
I think it's awesome that so many
people are experiencing that.

00:07:47.417 --> 00:07:50.557
I think that's, like, great because
these are things that, they're just,

00:07:50.757 --> 00:07:54.655
I think for most people, they're just
so boring- That you would never get to

00:07:54.686 --> 00:07:58.186
Yeah, actually, like, handling them or,
or dealing with them, but, you know, your

00:07:58.195 --> 00:08:00.356
agent, Muse, can just do that for you.

00:08:00.416 --> 00:08:04.186
And then they're saving time, which I
think for, for lots of people, I think

00:08:04.215 --> 00:08:06.885
there are always experiences that are
just way more frustrating than they should

00:08:06.885 --> 00:08:09.025
be, like going to the DMV is one example.

00:08:09.176 --> 00:08:14.145
We actually, when we built Muse, we,
like, specifically tested it to see if it

00:08:14.145 --> 00:08:18.805
could deal with the DMV website because
that was, like, the pinnacle we thought

00:08:18.805 --> 00:08:23.835
of, like, the most frustrating experience
for any human is dealing with the DMV.

00:08:24.095 --> 00:08:29.095
So we kne- we needed to have Muse, like,
be able to, to do well in that situation.

00:08:29.116 --> 00:08:31.245
We've seen people use it for,
like, yeah, navigating the

00:08:31.256 --> 00:08:32.615
healthcare system's a big one.

00:08:32.625 --> 00:08:36.486
Medical claims is, like, a huge one
that, like, most people just… It's,

00:08:36.486 --> 00:08:40.155
like, so complicated and annoying that
it's just, like, incredibly frustrating.

00:08:40.185 --> 00:08:43.466
We've seen people, like, use it
to help run their small business.

00:08:43.525 --> 00:08:46.466
We've seen a lot of people, like,
use it to help run their social media

00:08:46.466 --> 00:08:50.365
even, and I think the medical system
is a really good example 'cause

00:08:50.715 --> 00:08:53.085
it's, like, so hard to understand.

00:08:53.326 --> 00:08:56.285
And I think the, the you know, one
of the best ways to put it is, like,

00:08:56.285 --> 00:08:57.725
I think the cases where people…

00:08:58.125 --> 00:09:01.386
where it's, like, a huge lifesaver are
the cases where, like, otherwise you

00:09:01.386 --> 00:09:05.386
would be at your computer, you'd have
to Google, like, 50 different things.

00:09:05.386 --> 00:09:08.436
You have to read, go to, like, 100
different websites and try to figure

00:09:08.436 --> 00:09:11.925
out what the hell is going on, and
it couls, it's just, like, this

00:09:11.995 --> 00:09:15.655
incredibly frustrating experience,
or you could have your Muse do it.

00:09:15.826 --> 00:09:22.022
I also think about how many things
I suspect are made difficult

00:09:22.022 --> 00:09:25.202
and time-consuming on purpose
in order to frustrate me so much

00:09:25.202 --> 00:09:26.422
that I don't get to the end?

00:09:26.682 --> 00:09:30.902
How many claims are actually designed
to be that hard so that I give up?

00:09:30.942 --> 00:09:36.471
And I wonder what effect more
people having more patience because

00:09:36.471 --> 00:09:40.021
they have support will have on
that kind of systems engineering.

00:09:40.352 --> 00:09:44.842
One of the, the little memes that I
made of the little Muse was, uh, Muse in

00:09:44.842 --> 00:09:47.252
Hood, which is like a Muse Robin Hood.

00:09:47.512 --> 00:09:52.701
But I think there's, like, a real truth
to this, which is there are lots of things

00:09:52.742 --> 00:09:58.212
that exist in the world that are, to
your point, meant to be hard so that mo-

00:09:58.251 --> 00:10:02.111
like, uh, canceling subscriptions is one
of the best examples, where there's some

00:10:02.111 --> 00:10:05.171
subscriptions where you have to, like,
click on one thing and click on another

00:10:05.172 --> 00:10:09.151
thing, and then click on, like, the little
piece of text, and then not click the big

00:10:09.161 --> 00:10:10.711
red button, but click the other button.

00:10:10.861 --> 00:10:14.522
And, like, that's just, like, the perfect
example where there's lots of cases

00:10:14.522 --> 00:10:19.671
where things are designed to be hard
for people, and I think, like, this is

00:10:19.731 --> 00:10:23.792
giving people the tools to be able to just
do those things seamlessly and easily.

00:10:24.091 --> 00:10:27.252
I got access to the calling, um,
and I had it make a call this

00:10:27.252 --> 00:10:28.752
morning, which it did successfully.

00:10:29.092 --> 00:10:30.811
What does it sound like on the other end?

00:10:31.192 --> 00:10:35.981
I think it's clear for the other end
that it is, like, an AI, and I think

00:10:35.982 --> 00:10:39.142
if you look, read the, you know, we
show, we show users the transcript.

00:10:39.142 --> 00:10:41.621
Like, if you see the transcript,
like, the beginning of the call I

00:10:41.622 --> 00:10:44.492
always think is really funny because
it's like, "Hi, I'm so-and-so.

00:10:44.631 --> 00:10:49.512
Um, I'm Cleo's assistant, and, uh,
this call is being recorded." And I

00:10:49.561 --> 00:10:53.312
think it's just, like, always this very
funny beginning of a call. "Hi, I'm

00:10:53.312 --> 00:10:55.291
Haley calling on Cleo Abrams' behalf.

00:10:55.971 --> 00:10:59.162
I'm transcribing our call for notes."
What are some examples of when it's

00:10:59.171 --> 00:11:03.032
messed up for you or when it's, you feel
like categories it struggles for still?

00:11:03.372 --> 00:11:08.141
One thing that we're, we're always
trying to improve is the memory

00:11:08.262 --> 00:11:10.439
of these Of these agents, right?

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:13.939
And this is still one of the more
unsolved problems, I think, for a lot

00:11:13.939 --> 00:11:17.660
of these agents, is having the-- You
know, the, the memory of the agents

00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:19.449
is, like, not like that of a human.

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:23.269
Um, sometimes it can feel like it's
like that, but it is really different.

00:11:23.279 --> 00:11:25.780
So that's one area where we're,
like, always trying to improve.

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:28.360
We always wanna, wanna figure out
how to make the agents better.

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:32.069
And then, like, we want them
to be able to continue doing

00:11:32.069 --> 00:11:33.620
more and more complex tasks.

00:11:33.659 --> 00:11:36.679
I, I find this really funny, but
there's, um, there's a lot of people

00:11:36.679 --> 00:11:40.029
who will just, like, ask their Muse,
"I want you to make me $10,000."

00:11:40.029 --> 00:11:43.620
And obviously, not everyone can just ask
their Muse to make them $10,000, but,

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:47.309
like, I think, I think these kinds of,
like, more amorphous, nebulous tasks,

00:11:47.309 --> 00:11:50.970
like, we want Muse to be able to at
least, like, build a plan and figure

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:54.779
out and, you know, you're gonna have to
do your part if you wanna make $10,000.

00:11:54.789 --> 00:11:59.859
But I think we wanna enable the, the agent
to do more and more kind of complex and

00:11:59.859 --> 00:12:02.030
esoteric tasks, um, to help the users.

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:07.060
There are some things where I feel
emotionally, even if Muse could do

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:08.939
them, I would prefer that they didn't.

00:12:08.949 --> 00:12:11.759
So for example, if I'm texting
a friend and I find out that

00:12:11.759 --> 00:12:14.849
their Muse agent is responding to
me, I would be offended by that.

00:12:15.189 --> 00:12:18.689
Are there categories of things that
you think explicitly Muse should

00:12:18.699 --> 00:12:23.449
not do that are sort- just, like,
extremely deprioritized, not because

00:12:23.449 --> 00:12:26.929
they're technically that hard, but
because socially, not the point?

00:12:27.509 --> 00:12:32.556
A lot of what we care about is, like having Muse kind of like clear the way

00:12:32.796 --> 00:12:36.336
such that you can spend more time on
the things that you want to do and the

00:12:36.336 --> 00:12:38.616
things that you enjoy doing, and the
things that are, like, valuable to you.

00:12:38.695 --> 00:12:44.876
We generally speaking want to figure out
how to make someone's Muse

00:12:44.916 --> 00:12:46.785
is like a different entity than them.

00:12:46.976 --> 00:12:48.786
That's why it's like a little cute guy.

00:12:49.396 --> 00:12:53.796
Um, uh, and, and we want to, over
time, continue making that more and

00:12:53.796 --> 00:12:57.616
more clear because, um, to your point,
like it is socially weird if you're

00:12:57.616 --> 00:13:00.506
like you think you're talking to your
friend and it just happens to be the

00:13:00.506 --> 00:13:03.265
little Muse, the little Muse guy-
Yeah behind the, behind the curtain.

00:13:03.556 --> 00:13:07.435
Speaking of what they look like, one
of the things I've visited humanoid

00:13:07.445 --> 00:13:10.396
robots before, and one of the things
they spend a lot of time thinking about

00:13:10.396 --> 00:13:13.385
is how to make it cute so that you like
them and you want them in your home,

00:13:13.385 --> 00:13:14.546
or at least you're not afraid of them.

00:13:14.646 --> 00:13:17.995
What did Muse look like in previous
iterations, and how did you

00:13:17.995 --> 00:13:20.176
decide on that particular guy?

00:13:20.385 --> 00:13:22.295
I think this is, like, one
of the most shocking things.

00:13:22.675 --> 00:13:27.975
What the Muse looks like now is what he
looked like in the very first prototype.

00:13:28.126 --> 00:13:32.025
It's kind of one of these, like,
rare things where the, the very

00:13:32.025 --> 00:13:35.536
first version was just perfect and
then just, like, grew on all of

00:13:35.536 --> 00:13:36.856
us, and we ended up going with it.

00:13:36.905 --> 00:13:41.256
We had lots of debates about it, uh,
which are, you know, in retrospect will

00:13:41.256 --> 00:13:44.615
be some of my funniest meetings of like,
you know, a bunch of serious adults

00:13:44.626 --> 00:13:50.195
getting in a room, like arguing about
this cute little, um, you know, character.

00:13:50.435 --> 00:13:54.435
But, uh, but yeah, I mean, I think we
were like, is it not serious enough?

00:13:54.435 --> 00:13:56.056
Is it, like, too playful?

00:13:56.175 --> 00:13:57.416
Does he need to be more colorful?

00:13:57.416 --> 00:13:59.295
Like, these were, like, all
things that we asked the questions

00:13:59.295 --> 00:14:00.185
on, and we looked at them.

00:14:00.185 --> 00:14:04.176
We're like, "No, he's perfect."
What is the big vision here?

00:14:04.185 --> 00:14:07.486
I sometimes struggle when talking to
AI leaders to push people to think

00:14:07.495 --> 00:14:08.905
about more than, like, five years out.

00:14:09.265 --> 00:14:12.696
But what is, what is the vision here?

00:14:12.705 --> 00:14:16.986
Is it that Muse becomes my layer
on everything on the internet?

00:14:17.005 --> 00:14:21.106
Is it, like, map this out for me if
it goes the way that you hope it does.

00:14:21.695 --> 00:14:28.095
What we really want Muse to be over time
is kind of this, like, general manager,

00:14:28.096 --> 00:14:33.225
so to speak, that helps you accomplish
all the things you want out of life.

00:14:33.255 --> 00:14:40.635
So, like, I think, I think one of the
kind of tragedies of the modern world is

00:14:40.636 --> 00:14:48.675
that, like, um, most people have wants
and dreams, um, that can range from,

00:14:48.675 --> 00:14:53.705
like, really big ideas, like, hey, I want
to start a company, or I want to cure

00:14:53.705 --> 00:14:58.156
disease, or I wanna save the planet, all
the way to, you know, small wants that are

00:14:58.156 --> 00:15:02.155
like, hey, I wanna spend more time with my
family and, uh, I want to eat healthier.

00:15:02.155 --> 00:15:07.255
And, you know, um, and, and there's
a whole range, but for most people,

00:15:07.255 --> 00:15:11.486
I think there's just so many-
barriers and burdens in the way.

00:15:11.516 --> 00:15:16.266
What I really hope it accomplishes is
help every single one of the eight billion

00:15:16.266 --> 00:15:21.086
people all around the world be able to,
uh, accomplish their wants and dreams.

00:15:21.516 --> 00:15:27.126
And, um, I think this is like, you know,
if we are able to accomplish that, like,

00:15:27.275 --> 00:15:29.425
I think the world will seem insane.

00:15:29.435 --> 00:15:32.786
That's what we really want Muse to
be and what, what we think it can, it

00:15:32.786 --> 00:15:34.745
can be, uh, if we keep working at it.

00:15:35.185 --> 00:15:38.545
You know, it's really nice to hear
you talk about giving people time back

00:15:39.126 --> 00:15:42.555
because for a really long time I have
felt, and I think a lot of people have

00:15:42.555 --> 00:15:49.636
feel, have felt like So much of the
financial incentive of most tech products

00:15:49.636 --> 00:15:54.896
that I use, especially the ones that
are ad backed, is to take my time, is to

00:15:54.896 --> 00:15:57.015
distract me from the things that I want.

00:15:57.336 --> 00:15:58.385
Muse is free right now.

00:15:58.396 --> 00:16:00.546
It probably will have ads in the future.

00:16:00.576 --> 00:16:05.245
Is there a tension between giving
people their time back and having an

00:16:05.375 --> 00:16:11.815
ads-based business, or are there other
incentives about, like, Muse steering

00:16:11.816 --> 00:16:15.555
me down a path that isn't quite what I
said I wanted because it's ad backed?

00:16:15.555 --> 00:16:18.205
How do you add ads to a product like this?

00:16:18.555 --> 00:16:21.755
First off, like, I think
right now it's, it's free.

00:16:21.785 --> 00:16:23.096
There are subscription tiers.

00:16:23.155 --> 00:16:28.096
I think we think that there's plenty
of, of, um, business models that

00:16:28.096 --> 00:16:33.115
are really aligned with user around,
like, for example, if they, um, if

00:16:33.116 --> 00:16:37.336
they're buying things using Muse or
Muse is saving them money or, um, Muse

00:16:37.336 --> 00:16:41.405
is making them money, like figuring
out a way to, to take some small fee

00:16:41.405 --> 00:16:43.225
on, on those kinds of transactions.

00:16:43.465 --> 00:16:49.515
Um, so I wouldn't say we're spending
that much time thinking about, um,

00:16:49.575 --> 00:16:51.495
you know, ads necessarily right now.

00:16:51.816 --> 00:16:54.796
That being said, like, I don't think
ad-based models are necessarily

00:16:54.805 --> 00:16:58.155
synonymous with, like, you
needing to take all of your time.

00:16:58.155 --> 00:17:00.995
I think Google Ads are a really
good example of this, where, yeah.

00:17:01.365 --> 00:17:05.295
when I go to Google, my, my dream is
to get in and get out, and I don't

00:17:05.295 --> 00:17:07.605
think Google's really trying to stop
me from getting in and getting out.

00:17:07.895 --> 00:17:11.395
But they've built something obviously
pretty incredible, and so, yeah, I don't

00:17:11.405 --> 00:17:13.245
think it's a necessity by any means.

00:17:13.395 --> 00:17:18.575
Um, I think, again, I think if we
actually accomplish this thing that

00:17:18.575 --> 00:17:24.175
I'm saying, which is people are using
Muse to, uh, really, like, do the

00:17:24.185 --> 00:17:26.525
things that they want to do and do…

00:17:26.585 --> 00:17:30.916
and, like, really, like, you know,
in a non-corny way, like, accomplish

00:17:30.916 --> 00:17:34.825
their dreams, I think it's, I, you
know, I think there's gonna be a lot

00:17:34.825 --> 00:17:37.305
of ways to, to make that sustainable.

00:17:38.265 --> 00:17:43.056
So when I think about mapping out a
future to get there, to this optimistic

00:17:43.065 --> 00:17:46.446
vision, one of the most important
things is what do we need to get

00:17:46.446 --> 00:17:49.475
right, what challenges do we need
to avoid in order to make that true?

00:17:49.865 --> 00:17:54.706
I want to hear you use Muse and how you've
dealt with these challenges at Muse as

00:17:54.706 --> 00:17:58.405
an example of larger questions in AI.

00:17:58.425 --> 00:18:02.506
I think everything that I'm
about to say applies to basically

00:18:02.506 --> 00:18:04.905
every AI product, and I would be
curious if you disagree with that.

00:18:05.095 --> 00:18:07.216
I think there are basically
three levels here.

00:18:08.015 --> 00:18:10.505
Level one is privacy.

00:18:11.205 --> 00:18:14.708
So you say, "I download Muse." And
then I get to the point where it

00:18:14.718 --> 00:18:18.248
asks to be connected to my Gmail,
and I say, "Whoa, whoa, whoa, I'm

00:18:18.248 --> 00:18:19.718
not sure that I wanna do that.

00:18:19.758 --> 00:18:23.727
Like nope, nope, I'm out." That, at
the lowest level, is a concern about

00:18:23.748 --> 00:18:27.708
the relationship between the user
and Muse itself, and therefore Meta.

00:18:27.768 --> 00:18:30.388
So what is Meta going to
do with my information?

00:18:30.467 --> 00:18:32.348
Could hackers get that information?

00:18:32.678 --> 00:18:36.187
And in some countries, can governments
get that information somehow?

00:18:36.397 --> 00:18:38.308
I know that you've spent a lot
of time thinking about this.

00:18:38.397 --> 00:18:41.807
I'd like to get as detailed as we
possibly can here for a lay audience.

00:18:42.227 --> 00:18:43.708
What is the sentinel agent?

00:18:43.717 --> 00:18:46.217
What is Muse Confidential and Secure VM?

00:18:46.797 --> 00:18:50.238
How do you think about this in
terms of what can Muse see and not

00:18:50.238 --> 00:18:54.677
see, and things people should know
about this first level of privacy?

00:18:54.928 --> 00:18:58.498
Muse is built on top of the, the
secure VM, the Muse secure VM.

00:18:58.777 --> 00:19:05.188
And, um, that is a completely sandboxed
virtual machine, uh, or think about it as

00:19:05.188 --> 00:19:07.407
like a, a mini sandbox computer- Mm-hmm

00:19:07.417 --> 00:19:09.897
that is sort of isolated.

00:19:10.027 --> 00:19:13.647
The information that you share
with, with Muse is stored in

00:19:13.647 --> 00:19:15.028
that little secure computer.

00:19:15.028 --> 00:19:16.877
It is not stored elsewhere.

00:19:17.047 --> 00:19:20.577
Um, and that little secure
computer is sort of like your home,

00:19:20.617 --> 00:19:22.007
your Muse's home, so to speak.

00:19:22.188 --> 00:19:29.257
Inside that secure VM, you have a, uh…
we have a very, uh, pretty sophisticated

00:19:29.258 --> 00:19:34.437
security architecture because, you
know, we knew that privacy and security,

00:19:34.457 --> 00:19:38.328
frankly speaking, were gonna be some of
the most important things for, for most

00:19:38.328 --> 00:19:40.008
consumers to, to get them to adopt it.

00:19:40.288 --> 00:19:43.817
So as you mentioned, we built
a sentinel, a, uh, this sort of

00:19:43.817 --> 00:19:45.318
architecture with a sentinel agent.

00:19:45.487 --> 00:19:51.247
So the way it works is that, uh,
the, the model or the AI agent on

00:19:51.248 --> 00:19:56.067
the inside, as it's, um, going about
and, and doing its work, whenever it

00:19:56.077 --> 00:19:59.714
wants to, uh- communicate outwards.

00:19:59.754 --> 00:20:04.744
So it, it wants to take any information
and send it out, either to do, you know,

00:20:04.904 --> 00:20:09.194
um, fill it out on a website, or send
a message, or make a phone call, or,

00:20:09.304 --> 00:20:13.594
you know, any case where it would take
information from outside of that virtual

00:20:13.594 --> 00:20:19.014
machine, there's a separate agent, uh,
the sentinel, that's watching and making

00:20:19.014 --> 00:20:23.433
sure that, um, it's kind of like a,
almost like a traffic cop or something.

00:20:23.433 --> 00:20:27.984
It's just, like, making sure that, you
know, uh, there's nothing, uh… It's

00:20:27.984 --> 00:20:31.144
not saying like, "Oh, whoa, whoa, whoa,
that information, like, that's your

00:20:31.763 --> 00:20:34.794
credit card number or Social Security
number," or whatever it might be.

00:20:34.794 --> 00:20:35.994
Like, do not share that.

00:20:36.004 --> 00:20:37.584
That should stay, that should stay inside.

00:20:37.824 --> 00:20:42.554
And then the last thing that we do is,
um, uh, a lot of human loop verifications.

00:20:42.574 --> 00:20:48.034
So whenever Muse wants to connect
to a new website or send information

00:20:48.034 --> 00:20:51.493
to a new website, um, there will be,
like, a little dialogue that pops up.

00:20:51.703 --> 00:20:55.943
Which asks the user, like, "Hey, are
you okay with Muse contacting this

00:20:55.943 --> 00:20:58.473
website or sending information to
this, this website," and whatnot.

00:20:58.513 --> 00:21:03.383
And, um, and we, uh, and some people
have said we're, like, really annoying

00:21:03.383 --> 00:21:07.473
with it, that we like, are constantly
asking people to approve where

00:21:07.474 --> 00:21:08.474
their Muse is sending information.

00:21:08.474 --> 00:21:12.423
But we just wanna make sure that, you
know, people know exactly what their

00:21:12.423 --> 00:21:15.503
Muse is interacting with and, and,
and understand that really deeply.

00:21:15.563 --> 00:21:20.183
Then in addition to that, we are building,
um, and will release soon, uh, what's

00:21:20.184 --> 00:21:25.343
called the confidential VM, which will
be an even more secure and private

00:21:25.453 --> 00:21:31.123
offering where it's even more encrypted,
it's even more secure, um, and, uh, it's

00:21:31.123 --> 00:21:36.233
actually designed by, uh, the creator,
the person who built WhatsApp encryption

00:21:36.243 --> 00:21:39.473
and, and helped build other en- you
know, encryption on other major messaging

00:21:39.513 --> 00:21:41.103
platforms, like Moxie Marlinspike?

00:21:41.133 --> 00:21:45.073
Moxie Marlinspike, exactly, who's, I
would say, one of the real luminaries in

00:21:45.074 --> 00:21:47.484
the industry about security and privacy.

00:21:47.484 --> 00:21:50.164
And that's something that, that will
be an additional offering that we,

00:21:50.333 --> 00:21:51.603
uh, are going to be releasing soon.

00:21:52.093 --> 00:21:53.713
Why launch without it?

00:21:53.864 --> 00:21:57.083
Why launch first with secure as
opposed to waiting for confidential?

00:21:57.742 --> 00:21:58.792
I think I got that right, right?

00:21:58.812 --> 00:21:59.712
Secure is the one now.

00:21:59.712 --> 00:22:00.062
Yeah.
Confidential's coming later.

00:22:00.062 --> 00:22:02.262
Sec- secure is the one now,
confidential's the one later.

00:22:02.312 --> 00:22:05.882
There's a lot of technical challenges to
get the confidential VM working right,

00:22:05.921 --> 00:22:10.971
and even, even when it's released, there
are, there are going to be trade-offs

00:22:11.091 --> 00:22:13.091
associated with the confidential VM.

00:22:13.122 --> 00:22:16.372
Like, because of how it's architected and
because of various technical trade-offs

00:22:16.372 --> 00:22:19.481
you have to make, it might be a little bit
slower, it might have other challenges.

00:22:19.482 --> 00:22:24.761
So, um, we wanted to get a great
experience that we knew already

00:22:24.761 --> 00:22:27.701
we'd invested a ton of resources
into safety and security.

00:22:27.902 --> 00:22:34.531
Um, you know, we actually delayed the
launch of Muse for many months to get the

00:22:34.681 --> 00:22:39.701
safety and security exactly right, um,
before, before ultimately releasing it.

00:22:40.001 --> 00:22:43.252
Uh, so we feel really, really good
about what we, what we have released.

00:22:43.492 --> 00:22:47.211
That being said, we're always trying to
push the, you know, push the envelope

00:22:47.211 --> 00:22:50.541
on how to make it even more secure,
and, and we'll be, you know, coming

00:22:50.541 --> 00:22:51.581
out with a confidential VM soon.

00:22:51.891 --> 00:22:56.272
Does that mean when the confidential VM
comes out, if a government, and there

00:22:56.281 --> 00:22:58.891
are many places around the world where
people might be using Muse where they're

00:22:58.902 --> 00:23:03.151
worried about this, if a government
asks for data similar to WhatsApp, Meta

00:23:03.291 --> 00:23:05.452
cannot give it, even if they wanted to?

00:23:05.771 --> 00:23:09.842
Yeah, so our goal with the confidential
VM is to give the same guarantees that

00:23:09.842 --> 00:23:11.481
we're able to offer for WhatsApp, yeah.

00:23:11.901 --> 00:23:14.561
Okay, so this is moving into level two.

00:23:14.931 --> 00:23:20.781
Let's say I do decide to give Muse
all of my information, and I say,

00:23:20.871 --> 00:23:26.422
"I want you to do X task." Let's say
I say, "I really want you to get my

00:23:26.422 --> 00:23:29.961
grandmother an appointment at this
one specific doctor that's really

00:23:29.962 --> 00:23:30.991
hard to get an appointment with.

00:23:30.992 --> 00:23:36.022
Could you go do that?" And it goes
out, and it tries all of the ways to

00:23:36.022 --> 00:23:39.591
book appointments, and it, you know,
tries, you know, secret ways that I

00:23:39.591 --> 00:23:42.631
don't know about through discounts
or whatever, and then it reaches a

00:23:42.631 --> 00:23:44.442
point where it can't do that anymore.

00:23:44.812 --> 00:23:48.822
It's really important to me that it not
do things like give the doctor a bunch of

00:23:48.911 --> 00:23:52.341
private information about me, like what's
in my bank account, to try and bribe it

00:23:52.562 --> 00:23:56.911
or find out information about the doctor
to try and blackmail the doctor, right?

00:23:56.931 --> 00:24:00.211
From a human perspective, this is just,
"Do the task that I asked you to do,

00:24:00.312 --> 00:24:05.421
but don't break any laws, and also don't
break any ethics or etiquette rules

00:24:05.422 --> 00:24:08.692
that aren't literally laws, but it would
not be cool if you emailed the doctor

00:24:08.762 --> 00:24:10.861
100 times," or something like that.

00:24:11.111 --> 00:24:14.972
Internally, my understanding is, and we
use a lot of new words, I think, for AI.

00:24:15.361 --> 00:24:18.051
Mostly this is, like,
laws, etiquette, ethics.

00:24:18.661 --> 00:24:20.311
But internally, you call this discretion.

00:24:21.271 --> 00:24:25.801
How do I know that Muse has good
discretion, and how do you build a tool

00:24:26.191 --> 00:24:31.762
that does what you tell it to do, but
also understands the way you want that

00:24:31.762 --> 00:24:36.011
done without you having to say exactly
everything you don't want it to do?

00:24:37.102 --> 00:24:39.772
Yeah, so this is incredibly,
incredibly important.

00:24:39.862 --> 00:24:44.182
Um, I think, like, oftentimes, uh,
we wanna make sure that even when

00:24:44.182 --> 00:24:48.102
you don't explicitly tell it, like
you have to follow the law, and you

00:24:48.102 --> 00:24:53.192
have to, um, you know, follow general
social etiquette and, and ethics, as

00:24:53.192 --> 00:24:56.211
you mentioned, like you, we wanna make
sure the agent is able to follow those.

00:24:56.432 --> 00:25:00.292
Um, uh, as you mentioned, internally,
we think about this as some mixture of

00:25:00.292 --> 00:25:03.732
discretion and alignment ultimately,
like making sure that it is aligned

00:25:03.732 --> 00:25:08.672
with the user's intent as well as
aligned with the general values that

00:25:08.692 --> 00:25:13.202
we want to impart as a society on what
these agents do and, and how they act.

00:25:13.471 --> 00:25:16.942
There's kind of like many
layers that we try to ensure

00:25:16.942 --> 00:25:17.922
that these models are aligned.

00:25:17.922 --> 00:25:23.921
So one is, like in all stages of training,
we're ensuring that all of the data that

00:25:23.922 --> 00:25:27.421
is going into the models, that all the
techniques that we're using are ones that,

00:25:27.651 --> 00:25:31.292
um, ensure greater levels of alignment,
and then we measure it really rigorously.

00:25:31.311 --> 00:25:36.711
So, um, we're constantly evaluating how
aligned are the models by running them

00:25:36.711 --> 00:25:40.832
through, you know, thousands of different
scenarios and seeing exactly how they

00:25:40.832 --> 00:25:42.412
respond in each of those scenarios.

00:25:42.451 --> 00:25:47.621
And then we do lots of red teaming, what
it's called, or basically, um, you know,

00:25:47.771 --> 00:25:52.212
we get a bunch of really smart people,
and we say, "Do your worst. Try your

00:25:52.221 --> 00:25:57.521
hardest to get this agent to do something
bad. We give you permission to do that

00:25:57.521 --> 00:26:01.491
because we're, we're-- you guys are our,
our, uh, are the test bed that we're

00:26:01.491 --> 00:26:05.702
gonna know whether or not this, um, this
agent is, is, uh, properly aligned."

00:26:05.721 --> 00:26:09.362
So we go through many, many rounds
of that before ultimately, you know,

00:26:09.462 --> 00:26:11.781
launching or deploying any given model.

00:26:11.962 --> 00:26:15.316
You know, kind of taking a step
back, this is like a very, very

00:26:15.316 --> 00:26:20.516
hard problem in AI, and especially
as the AIs are getting better, this

00:26:20.546 --> 00:26:24.286
general problem is one of the things
that people are most concerned about.

00:26:24.526 --> 00:26:29.006
Um, because one of the patterns that
we've seen is, like, as the AI models

00:26:29.126 --> 00:26:34.876
are getting smarter and smarter and
smarter, um, you have to almost be more

00:26:34.876 --> 00:26:39.435
and more explicit about what are the
scenarios that you have to watch out for.

00:26:39.436 --> 00:26:42.466
It's kind of like how as kids
grow up, they learn how to

00:26:42.715 --> 00:26:44.585
cheat their tests in new

00:26:44.586 --> 00:26:47.715
and different ways, and then, you
know, they'll figure out a way to

00:26:47.716 --> 00:26:51.535
cheat that you're like, "Well, I
guess I didn't tell you exactly that

00:26:51.536 --> 00:26:54.936
that wasn't allowed, but now that I
see it, that's not allowed," right?

00:26:54.985 --> 00:26:57.615
And actually one of the biggest
challenges in the industry right

00:26:57.615 --> 00:27:00.006
now is, like, as these models are
getting really, really smart, how do

00:27:00.006 --> 00:27:01.415
we ensure that this doesn't happen?

00:27:01.696 --> 00:27:06.785
Um, but, but our approach really is,
is lots and lots of testing, lots

00:27:06.785 --> 00:27:11.166
and lots of evaluation, lots of red
teaming, um, applying a lot of effort

00:27:11.185 --> 00:27:14.615
in training and a lot of effort in the
development of the models to ensure

00:27:14.615 --> 00:27:16.236
that they're as aligned as possible.

00:27:16.426 --> 00:27:20.685
And then when the product is live and
when people are using Muse, if we get

00:27:20.686 --> 00:27:23.865
any user reports that are like, "Hey,
this did, like, a crazy thing," we

00:27:23.866 --> 00:27:27.355
take that so, so seriously, and we,
like, really try to make sure that

00:27:27.356 --> 00:27:28.425
we understand exactly what happened.

00:27:28.765 --> 00:27:31.736
At a macro level, this is
the paperclip example, right?

00:27:31.756 --> 00:27:37.065
When I was growing up, the sci-fi stories
that were scary was AI gets out with

00:27:37.065 --> 00:27:38.906
its own goals and starts doing things.

00:27:38.906 --> 00:27:40.825
This is Terminator and
The Matrix and whatever.

00:27:41.705 --> 00:27:46.195
But really more and more what I
hear smart people talking about is

00:27:46.205 --> 00:27:51.446
AI gets out, gets out with a warped
version of a goal that we gave it.

00:27:51.555 --> 00:27:54.255
This is, you know, make all the
paperclips, but, you know, please

00:27:54.256 --> 00:27:55.666
don't kill us all in the process.

00:27:56.076 --> 00:27:59.995
And I think Muse, the, the problems
that you're clearly dealing with

00:27:59.995 --> 00:28:03.856
and, and making sure that Muse
doesn't do are basically the tiny

00:28:03.856 --> 00:28:05.035
version of the paperclip problem.

00:28:05.055 --> 00:28:09.015
Like, please don't bribe my doctor or,
you know, if an AI is used in a robot,

00:28:09.025 --> 00:28:13.026
for example, wash my dishes, please don't
