00:28:13.026 --> 00:28:16.005
Like, there are things that are
really important to me that are not

00:28:16.005 --> 00:28:19.466
necessarily constrained in the prompt
and also not necessarily to your

00:28:19.466 --> 00:28:23.364
point in any, like, book of laws.

00:28:23.874 --> 00:28:26.784
That just seems like a really
difficult problem for everybody.

00:28:26.794 --> 00:28:31.054
What do you, what  like, zooming
out from Muse, how would you advise

00:28:31.054 --> 00:28:34.294
people to think about this problem
in a macro sense across all of AI?

00:28:35.284 --> 00:28:37.914
This is, by the way, yeah, I think
you articulate it really well.

00:28:37.933 --> 00:28:41.484
Another way that I like to think
about this is, like, the genie that,

00:28:41.484 --> 00:28:45.724
like, you know, grants your wish
but in, like, the exact wrong way.

00:28:46.114 --> 00:28:49.374
Like, I wanna live forever, and then,
like, traps you in some kind

00:28:49.374 --> 00:28:51.773
of, like, horrible situation for eternity.

00:28:52.034 --> 00:28:55.584
This is, like, in as you mentioned,
like, in some ways, the big problem.

00:28:55.593 --> 00:28:59.593
Like, how do we make sure these models,
um, are aligned going into the future?

00:28:59.943 --> 00:29:05.683
And there's a bunch of approaches
that, uh, we think may work.

00:29:05.733 --> 00:29:09.984
This is, I think, one of the most
open questions scientifically

00:29:10.044 --> 00:29:11.733
in AI, generally speaking.

00:29:11.733 --> 00:29:14.863
I think nobody knows exactly
the way to solve this problem,

00:29:14.884 --> 00:29:16.193
but there's a few ideas.

00:29:16.204 --> 00:29:22.433
So one idea is this, um, concept of,
uh, what's called scalable oversight.

00:29:22.614 --> 00:29:26.913
So the, the idea goes something like
this: so as you have smarter and smarter

00:29:27.034 --> 00:29:33.294
AIs, as long as the AIs are roughly as
smart as we are, we can kind of see what

00:29:33.294 --> 00:29:37.054
they're doing, and we can tell, like,
"Hey, that's a little bit off." And so

00:29:37.054 --> 00:29:38.914
we can, what's called provide oversight.

00:29:38.914 --> 00:29:42.724
Like, we can look at what the AIs are
doing and, and make sure that what

00:29:42.724 --> 00:29:44.273
they're doing is, is appropriate.

00:29:44.354 --> 00:29:50.763
The problem is when the AIs get a lot
smarter than we are, and we kind of

00:29:50.764 --> 00:29:53.083
look at what they're doing and it's
just, like, really confusing to us.

00:29:53.094 --> 00:29:54.704
Like, how do we deal with that situation?

00:29:54.994 --> 00:29:58.473
So there's this idea of scalable
oversight, which is basically as the

00:29:58.513 --> 00:30:02.733
AIs get smarter, we use a different
set of AIs to observe what they're

00:30:02.733 --> 00:30:04.323
doing and keep them in check.

00:30:04.744 --> 00:30:07.963
It's kind of like the police, for lack
of a better term, for, for the AIs.

00:30:07.994 --> 00:30:14.193
And then, uh, as you are building smarter
and smarter agents and smarter and smarter

00:30:14.194 --> 00:30:18.833
models, you also have to build smarter
and smarter, um, uh, policing agents or

00:30:18.834 --> 00:30:21.903
oversight agents or, um, uh, oversight AI.

00:30:22.064 --> 00:30:24.153
There's so many details that you
obviously have to get right, and this

00:30:24.154 --> 00:30:26.963
is such, like, a vague idea, but I
think something like this can work.

00:30:27.033 --> 00:30:32.463
Another one, which is, uh, which
is, this is kind of a fun- it almost

00:30:32.463 --> 00:30:33.883
sounds like sci-fi if I explain it.

00:30:34.183 --> 00:30:38.013
One of the big topics today is this
topic of recursive self-improvement.

00:30:38.414 --> 00:30:45.033
Which is this idea that, um, at some
point, the AIs are going to get so good

00:30:45.383 --> 00:30:50.253
at doing AI research themselves that
they can literally, you know, the AI

00:30:50.283 --> 00:30:57.264
agents can do research on their own to
improve, um, AI itself, and they can

00:30:57.264 --> 00:30:59.964
keep doing that, and keep doing that, and
keep doing that, and then the AIs just

00:30:59.964 --> 00:31:01.183
get better really, really, really fast.

00:31:01.213 --> 00:31:06.844
Well, when you have agents that are
able to do AI research, you can al-

00:31:06.874 --> 00:31:10.103
instead of applying them towards the
problem of making the AI smarter,

00:31:10.633 --> 00:31:13.273
you can apply them to the problem
of making the AIs more aligned.

00:31:13.523 --> 00:31:19.554
So you can say, "Hey, super smart AI
researcher agent, like, I want you to

00:31:19.554 --> 00:31:23.923
focus all of your time and attention
at making the AIs more aligned.

00:31:24.054 --> 00:31:27.173
And this is how I'm measuring
whether or not the AIs are aligned.

00:31:27.363 --> 00:31:30.664
This is, like, the, the red teaming
setup that we're gonna use to, we

00:31:30.664 --> 00:31:33.383
agreed we're gonna use that to measure
whether or not the AIs are aligned.

00:31:33.593 --> 00:31:36.363
And then I just, I want you to spend
all of your effort and attention

00:31:36.373 --> 00:31:37.584
on making the AIs more aligned."

00:31:37.843 --> 00:31:41.153
I actually think, you know, as sci-fi
as this sounds, like, it kind of sounds

00:31:41.153 --> 00:31:46.173
crazy, which is say, "Oh yeah, the AIs
are just gonna solve this AI alignment

00:31:46.173 --> 00:31:47.743
,this AI alignment problem for us.

00:31:47.973 --> 00:31:51.673
Um, I think there's, I think there's
real merit to, to that kind of approach.

00:31:51.713 --> 00:31:55.403
And then there's, there's a sort of
like third bucket, which is kind of a

00:31:55.453 --> 00:32:00.096
back to the basics version, which is-
Um, hey, the algorithms that we have

00:32:00.096 --> 00:32:06.016
now and the, the methods that we have
now, um, you know, while we don't fully

00:32:06.016 --> 00:32:11.465
understand them, we know that, uh, some
of these problems emerge, and we should

00:32:11.466 --> 00:32:15.749
go back to the basics to figure out what
are underlying tweaks to the algorithm,

00:32:15.749 --> 00:32:20.175
how do we improve the underlying
algorithm to, to make it more safe?

00:32:20.255 --> 00:32:25.225
I think intuitively, like, it should be
possible to create algorithms that, that

00:32:25.225 --> 00:32:28.485
don't have some of these behaviors, and
I think that's another really promising

00:32:28.485 --> 00:32:31.115
direction, which I think there's a bunch
of startups doing, and there's a bunch

00:32:31.115 --> 00:32:34.756
of, um, there's a bunch of research
efforts going towards is like what

00:32:34.756 --> 00:32:40.185
are more safe, uh, more generalizable
methods of solving this sort of

00:32:40.185 --> 00:32:41.966
like, you know, intelligence problem.

00:32:42.405 --> 00:32:47.285
I heard Mark say recently that at a
certain point, especially for consumer

00:32:47.286 --> 00:32:52.656
tools, it won't matter so much how
