00:40:44.484 --> 00:40:45.823
now and what people's options are.

00:40:46.424 --> 00:40:49.984
So I have some
visual, because obviously.

00:40:50.224 --> 00:40:56.024
So the basic way to think about this,
I think, is for everything that touches

00:40:56.024 --> 00:40:59.014
a consumer, there are things that
are checked before they ever touch

00:40:59.014 --> 00:41:01.974
consumers and on an ongoing basis,
and there are things that are checked

00:41:02.034 --> 00:41:04.993
only after they reach people's hands,
and it depends on how important the

00:41:04.994 --> 00:41:06.523
thing is and how risky and all that.

00:41:06.523 --> 00:41:11.334
So if you think about the specific
examples, nuclear power plants, the NRC

00:41:11.363 --> 00:41:15.913
approves nuclear power plants before
they go live, and then also has human

00:41:15.913 --> 00:41:19.933
beings at the sites checking them all
the time, which seems, and we'll, we'll

00:41:19.933 --> 00:41:23.943
get to this, seems pretty close to
what Dario is asking for in some ways.

00:41:24.013 --> 00:41:30.583
Then you have the FDA approves drugs
before they ever reach our hands, and

00:41:30.584 --> 00:41:32.324
then also checks them on an ongoing basis.

00:41:32.363 --> 00:41:34.954
Same thing with the FAA
checking airplanes, right?

00:41:34.954 --> 00:41:37.133
Like, these are all things
that are approved beforehand

00:41:37.133 --> 00:41:38.134
and then regularly checked.

00:41:38.234 --> 00:41:39.963
But there are other
high-risk things, right?

00:41:40.043 --> 00:41:40.653
Cars.

00:41:41.084 --> 00:41:44.473
The NHTSA offers federal guidelines.

00:41:44.563 --> 00:41:49.464
Manufacturers certify that they've met
those guidelines, and then the government

00:41:49.514 --> 00:41:51.564
purchases cars off the road to check them.

00:41:51.564 --> 00:41:55.083
And then you have things like supplements
that are actually under the purview of the

00:41:55.084 --> 00:41:57.494
FDA, but they're checked after the fact.

00:41:57.533 --> 00:42:01.434
Those are mostly covered by what, if
people are listening to, you know, the

00:42:01.434 --> 00:42:04.743
interviews with Jensen, for example, and
he's talking about liability and, like,

00:42:04.743 --> 00:42:08.114
people suing after the fact, after harm,
like, that's generally this category.

00:42:08.334 --> 00:42:12.543
Does this feel right to you as, like,
your, first, I would just want to check

00:42:12.553 --> 00:42:14.133
before I dive into how this applies to AI.

00:42:14.133 --> 00:42:16.753
Yeah.
I think this seems roughly right.

00:42:16.853 --> 00:42:17.393
Yeah.
Okay.

00:42:17.673 --> 00:42:20.124
So then we have… wait,
this is the fun part.

00:42:21.433 --> 00:42:21.983
Ha.

00:42:22.014 --> 00:42:24.143
So then we have all of our AI leaders.

00:42:24.444 --> 00:42:24.993
There you are.

00:42:27.063 --> 00:42:30.943
This is my assessment based on where
people have expressed themselves

00:42:30.953 --> 00:42:32.104
via their public statements.

00:42:32.493 --> 00:42:35.253
My understanding of where Dario
would say AI is, is he would

00:42:35.253 --> 00:42:36.204
say it's somewhere over here.

00:42:36.213 --> 00:42:41.893
He would say… Specifically, he's
asked for in-person, ongoing checks

00:42:41.913 --> 00:42:45.433
on models with physical human beings
at the sites, which sort of sounds

00:42:45.433 --> 00:42:46.864
like what we currently do for nuclear.

00:42:47.174 --> 00:42:50.263
And then you have, like,
Elon and Sam weighing in,

00:42:50.264 --> 00:42:51.504
saying, like, "Dario's right."

00:42:51.564 --> 00:42:54.144
I would actually say Zuck is
somewhere in the middle in terms

00:42:54.144 --> 00:42:56.153
of where he's saying AI is.

00:42:56.193 --> 00:42:58.603
I would say Jensen is
maybe over here right now.

00:42:58.603 --> 00:43:01.673
He's, based on his public statements
about generally there is a point of

00:43:01.673 --> 00:43:05.704
view that is like, "If it would hurt
people, don't ship it. We have incentives

00:43:05.704 --> 00:43:07.263
for that. That's what liability is."

00:43:07.273 --> 00:43:09.493
Like, there is a legal
infrastructure for that as well.

00:43:09.573 --> 00:43:12.213
If your product is not ready to
ship, don't ship the product.

00:43:12.443 --> 00:43:15.604
So my question is, can you help
me help people think about this?

00:43:15.883 --> 00:43:20.704
Like, when w- when they're hearing
news about how to regulate AI,

00:43:21.484 --> 00:43:22.483
how do you think about this?

00:43:23.544 --> 00:43:26.514
And how do you place yourself in this?

00:43:27.978 --> 00:43:30.348
mess. You know, you said it exactly right.

00:43:30.348 --> 00:43:36.357
It is so confusing because at face value,
what everyone's saying and what everyone's

00:43:36.408 --> 00:43:41.498
doing feel so crazily divergent.

00:43:41.758 --> 00:43:45.398
And so you're sort of in this world
where, do I believe what they say?

00:43:45.418 --> 00:43:46.568
Do I believe what they're doing?

00:43:46.578 --> 00:43:48.157
Like, where do people actually sit?

00:43:48.158 --> 00:43:49.418
What do they actually believe?

00:43:49.688 --> 00:43:51.827
Um, and I find myself asking this too.

00:43:51.848 --> 00:43:57.398
Like, I think it was, it's very funny
that, you know, um, the same people

00:43:57.398 --> 00:44:00.387
who are advocating for pacing the
frontier also are building a wet lab.

00:44:00.398 --> 00:44:06.028
Like, that's, I think, a really, um,
weird, uh, confluence of, of factors.

00:44:06.037 --> 00:44:11.527
So I think where, where I stand
is, like, ultimately, um, this

00:44:11.528 --> 00:44:16.907
technology will become more
and more and more, um, capable.

00:44:17.008 --> 00:44:22.497
And, uh, you know, part of what is
both scientifically very exciting,

00:44:22.497 --> 00:44:26.367
but also I think brings all these
questions into the foreground,

00:44:26.367 --> 00:44:29.437
is just how quickly AI has moved.

00:44:29.477 --> 00:44:35.357
Even over the past year, like a year ago,
most people, like, coded roughly by hand.

00:44:35.367 --> 00:44:38.787
They would, like, go into the, into
the code editor and write code.

00:44:38.928 --> 00:44:41.497
And now people are mostly
just talking to agents.

00:44:41.537 --> 00:44:45.788
So the speed at which everything
is moving is, is quite staggering.

00:44:46.077 --> 00:44:54.077
Um, and I think a lot of what the, a lot
of the concerns are, are heightened and a

00:44:54.077 --> 00:44:58.977
lot of the fear is heightened by the fact
that, um, like, if things are moving this

00:44:58.977 --> 00:45:00.178
fast, where is this gonna be in a year?

00:45:00.178 --> 00:45:01.338
Where is this gonna be in two years?

00:45:01.388 --> 00:45:05.458
The government and the public
react on a much slower time horizon

00:45:05.458 --> 00:45:07.007
than the pace of AI progress.

00:45:07.037 --> 00:45:11.087
And so by the time in one year or two
years or three years, or how many,

00:45:11.138 --> 00:45:15.567
however many years forward, when, um,
the public and government are really

00:45:15.567 --> 00:45:19.657
deciding what they wanna do, who knows,
like, where AI's gonna be at that

00:45:19.657 --> 00:45:21.227
point and how fast it's gonna move.

00:45:21.497 --> 00:45:25.597
So that's one of the things I think
explains some of the dialogue.

00:45:25.947 --> 00:45:30.307
Um, I think the other thing that kind
of explains some of this is the level of

00:45:30.307 --> 00:45:35.238
collection, collective action necessary
and the level of general mistrust,

00:45:35.437 --> 00:45:39.397
um, that exists between the various
I- AI leaders towards one another.

00:45:39.477 --> 00:45:43.267
And this is, like, well-covered, but
one major justification for folks

00:45:43.297 --> 00:45:47.957
is that, like, "Oh, I am responsible
and I would be responsible, but

00:45:48.168 --> 00:45:49.437
those guys aren't responsible."

00:45:49.438 --> 00:45:53.157
Whether those guys are, you know,
another AI company or China or,

00:45:53.287 --> 00:45:54.667
you know, who knows what boogeyman.

00:45:54.807 --> 00:46:00.177
So I can't slow down, otherwise those guys
who are irresponsible are going to, you

00:46:00.177 --> 00:46:01.157
know, who knows what they're gonna do.

00:46:01.668 --> 00:46:07.358
Um, and that basic logic, I think,
is very confusing because I think for

00:46:07.358 --> 00:46:13.568
most people, um, uh, the kind of, like,
common sense answer is, "Well, who

00:46:13.568 --> 00:46:14.487
cares what the other guys are doing?"

00:46:14.507 --> 00:46:17.287
Like, you have to, you have to
do what feels good to you, right?

00:46:17.317 --> 00:46:19.628
And, like, you should do
what's within your ethics.

00:46:19.628 --> 00:46:22.197
But I think that's also what
complicates a lot of this.

00:46:22.207 --> 00:46:27.116
So I think a lot of what- Is
necessary is, like, it's almost

00:46:27.116 --> 00:46:28.666
diplomacy is the right word.

00:46:28.666 --> 00:46:34.315
Like, how do we get this group of
people who all, who don't really trust

00:46:34.316 --> 00:46:38.216
each other that much, but all agree
that, like, this is really powerful

00:46:38.216 --> 00:46:42.815
technology, and we need to, we need to
figure out how to land it and how to,

00:46:42.895 --> 00:46:46.795
how to make sure that we develop it
responsibly and that things go well.

00:46:46.946 --> 00:46:49.686
Like, how do we, like,
make all of this work?

00:46:49.705 --> 00:46:54.705
And we're at the moment of, like, peak
insanity, I think, for that because

00:46:54.916 --> 00:46:58.586
it's, like, in the public, all these
problems are being, like, aired out.

00:46:58.625 --> 00:47:01.516
Like, we can all, we can tell that
nobody trusts each other, and we can

00:47:01.516 --> 00:47:05.835
tell that people are kind of, are-
are being really worried bef- like,

00:47:05.835 --> 00:47:08.826
maybe a little bit before the AIs
themselves are getting to the point

00:47:08.826 --> 00:47:09.985
where they're deserving of that worry.

00:47:09.985 --> 00:47:14.696
And so, I mean, I ultimately think the-
the most important thing is to, like,

00:47:14.866 --> 00:47:19.896
get everyone to agree to some common
foundation and base, and then develop some

00:47:19.896 --> 00:47:24.125
process by which we can all reevaluate
and consistently look at it and say,

00:47:24.256 --> 00:47:28.475
"Hey, we all agree actually that, you
know, we need to be doing this now, and

00:47:28.475 --> 00:47:31.845
we all need to be doing this other thing
now, and we all see, like, the models

00:47:31.845 --> 00:47:33.985
are getting powerful in- in certain ways.

00:47:33.985 --> 00:47:37.365
Like, let's all, let's all look at this
together." And I actually genuinely

00:47:37.366 --> 00:47:40.955
believe, for what it's worth, like,
that having these, like, collective

00:47:40.955 --> 00:47:45.666
dialogues is not only gonna be possible
among the AI leaders within America, but

00:47:45.675 --> 00:47:47.436
also gonna be possible internationally.

00:47:47.445 --> 00:47:52.145
Like, I think we have a, all have a shared
interest in making sure this technology,

00:47:52.336 --> 00:47:53.745
um, does not go horribly wrong.

00:47:54.116 --> 00:47:58.915
Yeah, I mean, the- the obvious next
question is the China of it all.

00:47:59.325 --> 00:48:02.305
I mean, you've talked about
how this is a big concern.

00:48:02.375 --> 00:48:08.695
People don't trust each other at the top
of these AI companies, but they all often

00:48:08.695 --> 00:48:12.436
say, "We have to go as fast as possible
because what if China gets there first?"

00:48:12.815 --> 00:48:16.915
I mean, you, Ted Cruz is out there saying,
like, "We would rather have US killer

00:48:16.915 --> 00:48:18.775
robots than Chinese killer robots."

00:48:18.796 --> 00:48:22.956
"If they're going to be killer robots,
I'd rather they be American killer

00:48:22.956 --> 00:48:24.995
robots than Chinese killer robots."

00:48:25.065 --> 00:48:29.615
Which I think scares a lot of people,
um … and also to say it mildly.

00:48:30.385 --> 00:48:35.836
Um, yeah, this is a spectrum of
American law, existing American law.

00:48:36.805 --> 00:48:41.632
This does not include any
global dynamics, not to mention,

00:48:41.772 --> 00:48:43.522
like, long-term collaboration.

00:48:43.842 --> 00:48:47.082
How do you evaluate this spectrum
given the China of it all, and,

00:48:47.082 --> 00:48:48.852
and what, what does that even mean?

00:48:48.862 --> 00:48:51.361
Like, how should I think about
China with respect to this dynamic?

00:48:51.702 --> 00:48:56.232
I've actually, like, personally  even
changed my own opinion on this matter.

00:48:56.351 --> 00:49:02.151
Like, I think in the, in the past I
was very worried about China, and I was

00:49:02.152 --> 00:49:05.911
very worried about what that dynamic
looked like, and I had read these,

00:49:05.912 --> 00:49:10.121
like, speeches that were given by PRC
officials in the past that talked about

00:49:10.121 --> 00:49:14.422
how they viewed AI as, like, this am-
this opportunity to leapfrog the West.

00:49:14.422 --> 00:49:17.022
And you know, these, these are the
concerning things when you read them.

00:49:17.401 --> 00:49:21.391
But as time has gone on, I think
that we're paying them out to be too

00:49:21.392 --> 00:49:25.782
much of a boogeyman when in reality,
um, they're going to see the same

00:49:25.791 --> 00:49:29.981
things that we see in terms of this
is powerful technology and we as

00:49:30.052 --> 00:49:31.672
a humanity need to get this right.

00:49:31.691 --> 00:49:36.131
There's going to be a lot of shared
goals in making sure that AI goes

00:49:36.131 --> 00:49:41.361
well, and I think, um and I think
it really is possible to arrive at

00:49:41.361 --> 00:49:43.311
some form of international cooperation.

00:49:43.511 --> 00:49:47.451
I think that the Chinese government,
like any government, has an interest

00:49:47.451 --> 00:49:52.601
in ensuring that there's stability
in their society, and that, um, they

00:49:52.601 --> 00:49:57.121
don't lose control of this technology,
and, uh, that overall, like, you

00:49:57.121 --> 00:50:01.062
know, things go well and we don't
have catastrophic existential risks.

00:50:01.062 --> 00:50:04.261
And so there's quite a bit of shared
ground that I think will en- will

00:50:04.261 --> 00:50:09.351
enable everybody to be rational
and think rationally and, and

00:50:09.411 --> 00:50:10.991
ultimately make the right decisions.

00:50:11.222 --> 00:50:16.892
Um, and even though it seems totally
insane right now and it seems like,

00:50:17.151 --> 00:50:21.581
it seems like in some ways that
feels impossible, I really have

00:50:21.581 --> 00:50:25.591
faith actually that, that as people
see more and more and learn more and

00:50:25.591 --> 00:50:28.902
more, more about the technology, um,
people will make the right calls.

00:50:29.701 --> 00:50:36.000
You have recently become One of the
20 most important people in that

00:50:36.000 --> 00:50:37.740
conversation in the United States.

00:50:37.830 --> 00:50:40.530
You've been very important in
AI in the last couple years.

00:50:40.530 --> 00:50:43.440
I mean, I think people remember seeing you
in the headlines when you came to Meta.

00:50:43.440 --> 00:50:46.190
I think this, you know,
isn't brand new to you.

00:50:46.229 --> 00:50:50.289
But in the last couple weeks, you
have designed and released a product

00:50:50.309 --> 00:50:56.010
that is being used by more people than
ChatGPT was in its first couple weeks.

00:50:56.039 --> 00:51:02.229
It feels like you have been thrust into
a new kind of part of this conversation,

00:51:02.559 --> 00:51:06.980
where frankly, people have will be asking
you more questions like, "Where exactly,

00:51:06.980 --> 00:51:11.239
Alex, do you fall on this spectrum?"
Like, what would you wanna see because

00:51:11.559 --> 00:51:12.920
you're one of the people responsible?

00:51:13.729 --> 00:51:17.069
Do you feel like you've come
to those conclusions yet?

00:51:17.209 --> 00:51:20.659
Yeah, I think, I think like anyone
who works on this technology, these

00:51:20.660 --> 00:51:25.279
are things that I've, like, been
thinking about for years and years.

00:51:25.329 --> 00:51:30.629
Truly, the way I think about what
I want to do and what I want to

00:51:30.629 --> 00:51:37.069
accomplish and how I want to shape this
conversation is, like, enable everybody

00:51:37.100 --> 00:51:42.190
to have a common dialogue, and then
get to the right place collectively.

00:51:42.569 --> 00:51:47.590
Like, I don't think at this point in the
conversation, it's, like, that helpful for

00:51:47.600 --> 00:51:53.039
me to say, "Oh, I believe this, and, you
know, you all should listen to me because,

00:51:53.219 --> 00:51:54.579
you know, I built Muse," or whatever.

00:51:54.700 --> 00:51:57.230
We already have a bunch of people.

00:51:57.579 --> 00:52:00.220
They all believe slightly
different things.

00:52:00.389 --> 00:52:04.909
Um, ultimately, this is a technology
that we need, like, collective action

00:52:04.910 --> 00:52:08.300
on, that everyone needs to agree on, that
we all need to get to the right place.

00:52:08.549 --> 00:52:14.190
So what I really care about is, like,
how do we facilitate and have the

00:52:14.200 --> 00:52:18.329
relevant dialogues in public that, and
private, that enable us to get to the

00:52:18.329 --> 00:52:20.769
point where we all agree on a path?

00:52:21.069 --> 00:52:28.124
And one of the very, um- strange
things for me is, like, I know a lot

00:52:28.124 --> 00:52:29.404
of these people, and I've met them.

00:52:29.453 --> 00:52:30.553
They're all brilliant.

00:52:30.834 --> 00:52:36.154
Um, but the degree to which certain
personal animosities are, like,

00:52:36.553 --> 00:52:42.124
actually defining what people
say and do and, like, how they're

00:52:42.124 --> 00:52:46.673
developing this really important
technology is, um, is insane to me.

00:52:46.754 --> 00:52:51.433
It is, in some ways, a very human
story that, like, feuds or, like,

00:52:51.433 --> 00:52:54.053
just the fact that, like, I really
don't trust this other person can,

00:52:54.383 --> 00:53:01.183
can, like, snowball into this, like,
civilizational level, um, problem.

00:53:01.363 --> 00:53:04.133
Um, but I don't think
that serves any of us.

00:53:04.133 --> 00:53:08.293
Like, I think ultimately we have to
all put our differences aside, put

00:53:08.293 --> 00:53:12.114
our histories aside, and just figure
out how we solve this together.

00:53:12.584 --> 00:53:15.774
It sounds very general, but
that's a fairly specific ask.

00:53:15.774 --> 00:53:18.914
I mean, there are constraints
that prevent that right now.

00:53:18.933 --> 00:53:22.403
There are antitrust regulations that
prevent a lot of those conversations

00:53:22.424 --> 00:53:26.703
from happening, and one of the
proposals, one piece of many proposals

00:53:26.703 --> 00:53:30.654
that Dario put forward is waivers
from those antitrust laws that would

00:53:30.654 --> 00:53:32.383
allow those kinds of conversations.

00:53:32.823 --> 00:53:35.883
I mean, tell me if I'm wrong, if there
are secret conversations happening,

00:53:35.883 --> 00:53:40.543
but I don't think there is, first, the
agreed-upon group in the United States

00:53:40.554 --> 00:53:44.214
that's having that conversation and
then also, like, how do we even begin

00:53:44.214 --> 00:53:46.133
to have those conversations with China?

00:53:46.154 --> 00:53:48.503
But actually, what don't I know?

00:53:48.533 --> 00:53:51.583
Is that something that's happening
in, in, you know, some private room

00:53:51.584 --> 00:53:54.313
somewhere, or is it that, like, we're
still at the beginning of that and

00:53:54.313 --> 00:53:55.573
we need to figure out how to do it?

00:53:56.163 --> 00:53:58.053
Um, I don't think it's
happening right now.

00:53:58.234 --> 00:54:02.344
At the White House, there was this
dialogue, um, and this, these accords

00:54:02.363 --> 00:54:04.193
put forth, which are a real step.

00:54:04.253 --> 00:54:08.183
Like, I think there's, um, getting
everyone to agree to something

00:54:08.243 --> 00:54:09.903
is like, that's really big.

00:54:10.473 --> 00:54:11.873
Everyone agrees on something.

00:54:12.054 --> 00:54:16.423
And I think that one, that thing, which is
basically saying, "Hey, we're going to…

00:54:16.683 --> 00:54:20.513
Every company is going to set out and
develop what their internal controls

00:54:20.513 --> 00:54:23.323
and principles around developing this
technology look like," and then there's

00:54:23.323 --> 00:54:28.083
gonna be multiple layers of verification
on that from internal teams that verify

00:54:28.113 --> 00:54:32.973
to, uh, external auditors that validate
that they're following those controls to

00:54:33.153 --> 00:54:37.543
board-level committees that ensure they
are following those, those, um, controls.

00:54:37.543 --> 00:54:39.684
Like, I think that's a great step.

00:54:39.893 --> 00:54:42.923
Um, 'cause in, in many ways,
a lot of what's needed right

00:54:42.923 --> 00:54:44.193
now is just transparency.

00:54:44.203 --> 00:54:48.153
Like, we just need to ensure
that, you know, people are going

00:54:48.153 --> 00:54:49.574
about this sanely and rationally.

00:54:49.574 --> 00:54:56.162
And I think in some ways, like people
don't even trust that necessarily.

00:54:56.241 --> 00:54:58.281
As an industry, we have a
lot of trust building to do.

00:54:58.752 --> 00:54:59.612
I am an optimist.

00:54:59.612 --> 00:55:00.511
I think you're an optimist.

00:55:00.511 --> 00:55:01.712
Like, I think we're all optimists.

00:55:01.721 --> 00:55:04.602
Like, I really do think these
conversations will naturally happen.

00:55:04.682 --> 00:55:08.191
This last accord that I referenced,
like, that was in the White House.

00:55:08.201 --> 00:55:10.471
The government helped facilitate that.

00:55:10.481 --> 00:55:13.371
And so I think we're gonna
be seeing a lot more of that.

00:55:13.442 --> 00:55:14.531
It's gonna be bumpy.

00:55:14.731 --> 00:55:17.682
There will be moments of total
confusion and chaos and, like,

00:55:17.841 --> 00:55:19.031
what the heck is going on?

00:55:19.082 --> 00:55:20.591
But I see progress, too.

00:55:21.261 --> 00:55:23.741
I'm glad that we've spent a lot
of this conversation talking

00:55:23.741 --> 00:55:25.921
about safety and regulation.

00:55:26.991 --> 00:55:32.391
How do we bring it back now to the
optimistic vision of the future?

00:55:32.411 --> 00:55:36.732
I think the question a lot of people
will naturally have after a long

00:55:36.741 --> 00:55:41.721
conversation about trust and safety
is, why bother doing this at all?

00:55:42.091 --> 00:55:45.932
This is the moment in the SNL sketch
where it's, uh, you know, if there's

00:55:45.932 --> 00:55:49.241
a 10% chance that something will kill
you, but it'll probably make your

00:55:49.241 --> 00:55:50.622
life better, like, wouldn't you do it?

00:55:50.651 --> 00:55:54.011
And the other guy's like,
"No. No, I wouldn't." No.

00:55:54.021 --> 00:55:54.521
"Really?!"

00:55:55.011 --> 00:55:56.781
Can you help people answer that question?

00:55:56.781 --> 00:55:58.362
Like, why do this?

00:55:59.854 --> 00:56:06.444
Something that I have always felt was
a bit tragic was that I think, um,

00:56:07.144 --> 00:56:13.553
almost everyone when they're kids,
they have big dreams and big ideas, and

00:56:13.553 --> 00:56:17.973
they want to change the world, and they
want to save the planet, and they want

00:56:17.983 --> 00:56:22.314
to, you know, uh, write a book or make
a Broadway musical or make a movie.

00:56:22.323 --> 00:56:25.333
Like, they have really,
they have tons of hope.

00:56:25.444 --> 00:56:30.963
And then for some reason or another,
by the time that people finish school

00:56:31.043 --> 00:56:36.563
and they get a job, that, like, flame
kind of dies and people really, um,

00:56:36.894 --> 00:56:41.073
they stop dreaming big, and they stop
having big ideas, and hoping for big

00:56:41.073 --> 00:56:42.363
things, and hoping for lots of change.

00:56:42.363 --> 00:56:46.354
And especially today, like, I think a
lot of young people get really cynical,

00:56:46.403 --> 00:56:52.423
and they're like, "Oh, the world is
horrible, and it is, and I don't know

00:56:52.424 --> 00:56:57.003
why it's so insane in so many ways,
but I guess this is just the way it

00:56:57.003 --> 00:57:01.633
is." They lose faith that they have
an ability to enable that change.

00:57:01.753 --> 00:57:07.553
And I genuinely think one of the
promises of AI, and especially something

00:57:07.553 --> 00:57:12.493
like Muse where you give AI to every
single person in the world, is that

00:57:13.364 --> 00:57:18.453
this doesn't happen, that people can
have big ideas and big dreams and wants

00:57:18.513 --> 00:57:23.183
and, and goals that they can have as
little kids, they can have when they're

00:57:23.184 --> 00:57:28.353
teenagers, they can keep having through
adulthood, and that, you know, an AI

00:57:28.354 --> 00:57:33.434
super intelligent sidekick is there to
help them just make all of that happen.

00:57:33.613 --> 00:57:39.403
And that can be everything from, like,
big wants and big dreams, um, to, like,

00:57:39.533 --> 00:57:43.893
small wants like, "Hey, I just wanna
spend more time with my kids," or,

00:57:43.894 --> 00:57:47.493
"I just wanna spend more time with my
parents," or, "I just want to, like, eat

00:57:47.493 --> 00:57:54.113
healthier," um, all the way up to, "Hey,
I have this, like, crazy idea for, um,

00:57:54.313 --> 00:57:56.054
like, a, a museum that I wanna start."

00:57:56.073 --> 00:57:59.423
Like, my, my partner really
wants to make a museum of ice.

00:57:59.703 --> 00:58:03.194
I think it's, like, hilarious,
but, um, Ice is crazy.

00:58:03.194 --> 00:58:06.374
There's a whole amazing story
about the evolution of cold,

00:58:06.394 --> 00:58:08.734
of how we m- keep things cold-
Yeah … that starts with ice.

00:58:08.734 --> 00:58:09.234
It's pretty cool.

00:58:09.553 --> 00:58:10.383
Yeah, it's- I'd go to the museum.

00:58:11.214 --> 00:58:11.794
Exactly.

00:58:11.953 --> 00:58:15.873
And, and people have these ideas,
and then they just can't…

00:58:15.883 --> 00:58:17.903
Y- you know, in some ways
young people are right.

00:58:17.904 --> 00:58:23.743
Like, the world today is just kind
of a maze of, like, roadblocks and

00:58:23.764 --> 00:58:27.454
issues and challenges and logistical,
um, you know, uh, things that get in

00:58:27.454 --> 00:58:29.523
your way to make those things happen.

00:58:29.563 --> 00:58:33.733
And in a small way today, I'm seeing
Muse make this problem better.

00:58:33.803 --> 00:58:39.133
Like, I'm seeing people using it to
save time, save money, like, take

00:58:39.133 --> 00:58:43.443
the little stuff off their plate,
and just give them more and more

00:58:43.473 --> 00:58:46.854
agency, more and more freedom, more
and more of an ability to spend

00:58:46.854 --> 00:58:48.103
their time on things they want to do.

00:58:48.473 --> 00:58:54.182
And I think one of the promises of AI
that gets better and better and better,

00:58:54.232 --> 00:58:58.162
gets not only smarter but also more
aligned and more trustworthy, is that

00:58:58.452 --> 00:59:00.901
you can give it bigger and bigger ideas.

00:59:00.912 --> 00:59:04.392
And you can say, "Hey, I
wanna start a museum of ice.

00:59:04.651 --> 00:59:07.932
Like, tell me how to go about doing
this." And it'll tell you, "These are

00:59:07.932 --> 00:59:11.721
the buildings you can lease, and this
is who you can contact for funding

00:59:11.732 --> 00:59:13.061
who might be passionate about ice.

00:59:13.091 --> 00:59:16.962
And, um, this is, like, how you should
think about curating your exhibit and,

00:59:17.111 --> 00:59:20.582
you know, uh, what, what that might
look like." And I think for some people

00:59:20.582 --> 00:59:27.722
like you or me who have experienced
this, this, like, journey of having big

00:59:27.722 --> 00:59:32.571
ideas and then, like, going through all
the crazy maze of things to make that

00:59:32.572 --> 00:59:35.181
happen, it is, like, really magical.

00:59:35.181 --> 00:59:39.542
Mm. And it's really special, and I
think it's something that, um, you

00:59:39.542 --> 00:59:43.681
know… I think human stories of
people who have a dream and then

00:59:43.681 --> 00:59:47.031
accomplish those are the most, like…
are some of just the most beautiful

00:59:47.041 --> 00:59:48.681
things that I can possibly imagine.

00:59:49.021 --> 00:59:55.111
And I w- truly think with AI and, like,
everyone with a little Muse sidekick,

00:59:55.131 --> 00:59:57.471
like, more people can experience this.

00:59:57.501 --> 01:00:00.341
I- my hope is that everyone,
literally, like, all eight billion

01:00:00.342 --> 01:00:03.392
people in the world experience
that and have the ability to do it.

01:00:03.882 --> 01:00:08.731
And that is just, like, a very
special world to live in, I

01:00:08.741 --> 01:00:09.861
think, if we can make that happen.

01:00:09.882 --> 01:00:14.112
And I think more people will be able to
get what they want out of their lives

01:00:14.162 --> 01:00:18.621
and, and live more fulfilled lives,
and it'll be magical and exciting.

01:00:18.671 --> 01:00:22.542
And that's w- what I really think
the promise is for, for people.

01:00:22.542 --> 01:00:27.801
And I think part of what is so confusing
is like, um, you know, oftentimes people

01:00:27.802 --> 01:00:34.022
in AI, we say, like, "Oh, AI will cure
cancer." And I think everyone wants cancer

01:00:34.022 --> 01:00:39.802
cured, but every individual person
has something they want intensely, and

01:00:39.802 --> 01:00:44.002
they want even more than i- you know,
ironically speaking, a cure for cancer.

01:00:44.212 --> 01:00:47.802
Um, and so I think that's part of
the magic is that we talk about this

01:00:47.802 --> 01:00:48.992
thing, personal superintelligence.

01:00:48.992 --> 01:00:52.862
Like, it will help you at the thing
that you want and the thing that you

01:00:52.862 --> 01:00:56.631
dream of, and, um, and that will change
over your life, and you will be able to

01:00:56.632 --> 01:00:58.252
accomplish many dreams and many wants.

01:00:58.601 --> 01:01:02.801
And I think that's what's,
uh, the promise of, of AI.

01:01:02.802 --> 01:01:08.652
That dream has within it a belief,
I think, which is that people

01:01:08.652 --> 01:01:12.901
do actually have big dreams, and
that those big dreams will more

01:01:12.901 --> 01:01:14.542
often than not help other people.

01:01:14.971 --> 01:01:20.752
So when we hear people say AI will cure
cancer, sometimes perhaps they're talking

01:01:20.752 --> 01:01:24.922
about you prompt an AI and it thinks
up the cure for cancer, but much, much

01:01:24.922 --> 01:01:30.701
more likely it's the AI facilitates
the individual ambitions of millions

01:01:30.701 --> 01:01:34.322
of cancer doctors, and together we
accelerate the progress toward cancer.

01:01:34.351 --> 01:01:38.321
It's making me think, you know, when
I hear people talk about Muse and

01:01:38.322 --> 01:01:43.331
say like, "What do people need an
AI assistant for? People don't have

01:01:43.371 --> 01:01:47.421
those dreams." Like, what a limited
view of peop- of other people.

01:01:48.241 --> 01:01:53.061
You know, I don't know what those are,
and it's not up to you or me to say

01:01:53.731 --> 01:01:58.141
which dream is more worthy than the
other, to your point, big or small.

01:01:58.362 --> 01:02:04.051
My view on people is like they will have
moments of inspiration where at some

01:02:04.051 --> 01:02:09.951
point or another they will realize, like,
"Wait, I really wanna do this thing." I

01:02:09.951 --> 01:02:15.251
want everyone who has one of these ideas
to be able to tell their Muse, "Hey, I

01:02:15.252 --> 01:02:20.012
have this big idea." And their Muse will
just like secretly behind the scenes help

01:02:20.012 --> 01:02:21.311
them figure out how to make it happen.

01:02:21.601 --> 01:02:27.354
And I think part of this is also
fanning the flame, so to speak.

01:02:27.723 --> 01:02:32.413
Um, I think for a lot of people, they
will have, like, a small want, and then

01:02:32.413 --> 01:02:36.463
it'll happen, and then they'll dream a
little bit bigger, and then it'll actually

01:02:36.473 --> 01:02:39.074
happen, and they'll dream a little
bit bigger, and it'll actually happen.

01:02:39.114 --> 01:02:45.353
And, um, and as that goes on, people
will sort of, uh, like, realize that

01:02:46.113 --> 01:02:49.433
AI can be so much more, that Muse can
be so much more, that, you know, they

01:02:49.433 --> 01:02:51.793
can, um, that they can do so much more.

01:02:51.853 --> 01:02:53.473
This won't happen overnight.

01:02:53.583 --> 01:02:56.163
This is something that will take
time, and everyone will have to

01:02:56.163 --> 01:02:58.813
experience on their own, and I
really hope everyone experiences.

01:02:59.003 --> 01:03:03.653
Um, but I think over, like,
like, generations, I think can

01:03:03.654 --> 01:03:04.894
be, like, absolutely magical.

01:03:05.634 --> 01:03:09.824
You know, I think especially the way
that you're in the press and talked

01:03:09.833 --> 01:03:14.823
about, and you kind of naturally become
sort of a caricature of yourself.

01:03:14.893 --> 01:03:17.553
There's, like, the character
of you out there in the world.

01:03:17.654 --> 01:03:21.493
And this has helped me get to know
you better because there seems like a

01:03:21.513 --> 01:03:25.953
common theme in some of your answers,
which is Muse is a way to facilitate

01:03:26.213 --> 01:03:30.763
other people's dreams because you have a
belief in people and their goodness and

01:03:30.764 --> 01:03:35.083
their ability to improve the world and
just the sort of value of their ideas.

01:03:35.103 --> 01:03:38.823
And it seems like you are directing
Muse in some ways, but you're not

01:03:38.944 --> 01:03:43.073
saying, "This is for coding," or,
"This is for X specific task." You're

01:03:43.073 --> 01:03:44.583
watching and, and facilitating.

01:03:44.614 --> 01:03:50.574
And when I asked you where you fall on
this, your answer was, "I'm interested

01:03:50.574 --> 01:03:52.813
in the collective wisdom of the group.

01:03:52.813 --> 01:03:56.923
I'm interested in facilitating or being
part of a conversation between other

01:03:56.923 --> 01:04:02.473
people, that we have a lot of smart
people all around the world, and I

01:04:02.473 --> 01:04:06.403
don't feel like it would be that useful
to say, 'Here's exactly my opinion on

01:04:06.404 --> 01:04:08.183
where exactly this should be regulated.'

01:04:08.193 --> 01:04:11.984
I see the best outcome as coming
from a group conversation of many

01:04:11.984 --> 01:04:15.413
different people with their many
thoughts and, and wants." Do you

01:04:15.413 --> 01:04:16.294
agree with what I've just said?

01:04:17.166 --> 01:04:20.636
Yeah, I think 'cause there are
so many big questions about AI,

01:04:20.666 --> 01:04:23.636
and it is, like, something that
everyone has a point of view on

01:04:23.666 --> 01:04:24.886
and should have a point of view on.

01:04:25.186 --> 01:04:32.125
And, um, part of, I think, what's
amazing about democratic institutions and

01:04:32.126 --> 01:04:35.416
democratic countries and, and democracy
broadly speaking is that it matters

01:04:35.416 --> 01:04:38.255
what everyone thinks, and we have to
figure out how we, like, take this,

01:04:38.256 --> 01:04:44.326
like, complex, jumbled- Yeah … um, uh,
mess of what everyone thinks and channel

01:04:44.326 --> 01:04:46.535
that into the right thing happening.

01:04:46.816 --> 01:04:53.105
And another thing is, like, part of
what has held AI back, and I think

01:04:53.105 --> 01:04:57.685
part of the problem of AI, is how it
almost feels like the people building

01:04:58.186 --> 01:04:59.795
AI are talking down on other people.

01:04:59.826 --> 01:05:04.775
It's like- Yeah … "Hey, I am building
s- the smartest thing you've ever known.

01:05:05.055 --> 01:05:08.636
It's gonna be way smarter than you, and I
know exactly what we're gonna do with it."

01:05:08.975 --> 01:05:13.385
And I think rightfully, people
are like, "Wait, what?" Like Yeah.

01:05:14.365 --> 01:05:18.605
"What does anything you just said have
to do with me?" And, and like, um, "How

01:05:18.605 --> 01:05:21.525
is this even good for me?" And then
you're like, "Oh, it'll cure cancer."

01:05:21.526 --> 01:05:25.115
And they're like, "Okay, but what
else?" Like Yeah, do I believe you?

01:05:25.236 --> 01:05:25.805
Or, yeah.

01:05:25.935 --> 01:05:26.435
Yeah.

01:05:26.555 --> 01:05:30.455
And so I think that especially given
the moment that we're in, and to your

01:05:30.456 --> 01:05:34.336
point, like, how much fear there is
about AI and how much uncertainty there

01:05:34.336 --> 01:05:38.845
is, and how confusing everything that's
being said is, like, um, I think the

01:05:38.846 --> 01:05:42.026
most important things are, like, first,
let's have everyone experience it.

01:05:42.035 --> 01:05:45.866
Like, let's have everyone
actually, like, understand the hype

01:05:45.905 --> 01:05:47.025
Yeah … for be- lack of a better term.

01:05:47.185 --> 01:05:52.985
Like, feel what AI agents can do for
you and can do to make your life better.

01:05:53.386 --> 01:05:56.435
And that'll give you perspective
as to, "Oh, this is a technology

01:05:56.435 --> 01:05:59.786
that, yeah, it's definitely risky,"
and, and there's, there's tons of

01:05:59.786 --> 01:06:05.445
risk, don't get me wrong, "but it
also is really great, and I like it.

01:06:05.475 --> 01:06:09.745
And I wanna make sure at least some
form of it continues, and I can continue

01:06:09.745 --> 01:06:14.245
using." Um, all the way to, you know,
the, these, like, big questions and

01:06:14.245 --> 01:06:18.705
these big dialogues, like, how do we
just get everyone to a place where we all

01:06:18.706 --> 01:06:20.615
agree, and we can find a path forward?

01:06:20.645 --> 01:06:23.805
And I think I have enough humility
that, like, these guys are, you

01:06:23.805 --> 01:06:25.605
know, they're big, important people.

01:06:25.606 --> 01:06:30.765
Like, I don't know if I have much to add,
you know, um, given their points of view.

01:06:30.765 --> 01:06:35.205
But I just wanna make sure that
we I- facilitate is really the

01:06:35.205 --> 01:06:36.505
word that keeps coming to my mind.

01:06:36.505 --> 01:06:41.475
Like, how do we move quickly to
all collectively come to informed

01:06:41.475 --> 01:06:45.336
opinions and, um, and then come to a
process where we agree on something?

01:06:46.670 --> 01:06:49.870
This is the question I ask everyone at
the end of these HUGE* conversations,

01:06:50.250 --> 01:06:56.950
which is when I think about after I'm
dead and after people say that she

01:06:56.950 --> 01:07:03.910
was a really great family member, and
she loved her husband, and she loved

01:07:03.910 --> 01:07:07.980
her friends, I would want them to
talk about the mission of this show.

01:07:08.209 --> 01:07:12.710
I would want them to talk about how
I tried my best to help people see

01:07:12.859 --> 01:07:17.549
ways the future could go well because
I really believe, as it seems you do,

01:07:17.629 --> 01:07:23.449
in other people, in their perspective,
and when they see a vision of a future

01:07:23.449 --> 01:07:25.819
they believe in, that they're gonna
actually try and help make it happen.

01:07:25.819 --> 01:07:29.950
Like, that's the point of what I
spend all of my working hours doing.

01:07:30.690 --> 01:07:32.659
You're obviously just starting out.

01:07:32.780 --> 01:07:34.789
You've already had this huge impact.

01:07:34.909 --> 01:07:38.240
You have a lot farther to go, but
I'm curious when you think about how

01:07:38.240 --> 01:07:42.060
you wanna be remembered after you're
gone, what do you want people to say?

01:07:46.734 --> 01:07:49.913
First of all, what a nice, what
a nice, um, idea and concept.

01:07:49.994 --> 01:07:55.634
Um, I think, I think for me, I w- will
want people to say that the things that

01:07:55.634 --> 01:08:00.963
I built and the things I contributed
to, um, helped everyone get more out

01:08:00.964 --> 01:08:02.234
of what they wanted out of their lives.

01:08:03.214 --> 01:08:03.714
Beautiful.

01:08:03.994 --> 01:08:04.824
Thanks for doing this.

01:08:04.873 --> 01:08:05.373
Thank you.

